From 1ea7f129171d4e7c46845816503a869aa2b61d1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iam <iam@iashur.internal>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 06:39:04 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] 360 viewer: stereo audio playback via PipeWire's pa_simple,
 A/V sync

HELLO_XR_AUDIO=1 opens the file's audio stream (if any) alongside the existing
video decode, resamples it to S16/48000/stereo with swresample, and plays it
through pa_simple - the PulseAudio-compatible blocking API PipeWire's own Pulse
shim implements, the same output path the rest of this rig's audio already uses.
Head-locked only: the content this player is built around (get360.sh's android_vr
downloads) is plain stereo, never ambisonic, so there is no spatialization/HRTF to
do - just decode, resample, and hand the two channels to the sound card.

Unset (default): zero behavior change. No new thread, no codec/resampler/pa_simple
opened, no packet routing changed - the existing silent player is byte-for-byte
what it was.

Sync: audio becomes the master clock once active. pa_simple_write's blocking
pacing IS the real hardware/PipeWire clock; the audio thread tracks how many
frames it has written since a known anchor pts and corrects for pa_simple's own
reported latency (frames written but not yet audible), publishing the result as
an atomic the render thread's AcquireCurrentSlot() reads with no locking. Falls
back to the existing wall-clock*rate accumulator during the brief startup window
and for one beat after a loop seam or seek while the audio thread is mid-flush.

Loop/seek: DecodeLoop signals the audio thread (a new audioResetPending flag,
its own mutex/cv so a busy audio thread never adds latency to the 90Hz render
path) at the same points it already resets its own clockStarted/loopOffset - the
audio thread answers by flushing its packet queue, pa_simple's buffer, and
re-anchoring, so stale pre-seek/pre-loop audio never bleeds across the seam.

Pause: SetRate(0) leaves the audio thread holding a fully-decoded chunk rather
than writing it, after flushing pa_simple once on the transition so buffered
audio goes silent immediately instead of fading out over the buffer - matching
the grip-to-pause gesture's intent (patch 0005) to be instant.

Any failure along the way (no audio stream, codec/resampler won't open, no sound
server to talk to) is logged once and playback continues video-only - a demo
must not die because its audio track or output device didn't cooperate.

Build-verified only (`ninja hello_xr`, clean under -Wall plus this project's
-Werror=implicit-fallthrough/-Werror=undef/-Werror=missing-braces, links against
libswresample and libpulse-simple). Confirmed pa_simple actually reaches this
box's live PipeWire-Pulse sink with a standalone test (connect, write, latency
readback, drain, all clean) - the decode+resample+sync logic itself has NOT been
run end to end: that needs a real XR session (headset or Monado's simulated HMD
fallback), which is out of scope for this change per the project's own
human-verification rule for anything audiovisual.

CMakeLists.txt: libswresample added to the existing FFMPEG pkg-config group;
libpulse-simple checked for separately and optional - its absence just means
HELLO_XR_AUDIO has no effect, not a build failure.
